<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parcham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parcham ('Banner') was one of the two factions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a>. Drawn mainly from urban, Persian-speaking intellectuals, it backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a>'s coup of 1973, while its rival faction, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a>, was more radical. After the Soviet invasion of December 1979 Parcham held power under Karmal and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parcham.jpg" alt="Parcham" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Ericmetro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Afghanistan_(1980-1987).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
